---
id: org.dxos.plugin.laMetric
name: LaMetricPlugin
version: 0.1.0
---

Shows a Composer space on a LaMetric TIME: a progress bar while a task is running, the space's
statistics otherwise, cycled through the device's frames.

Unlike the Stream Deck, no companion application is needed. The device is an HTTP server on the LAN
and LaMetric's cloud is an HTTP server on the internet, and the two accept an identical widget
update, so this plugin pushes directly. Requests go through Tauri's HTTP client because neither
endpoint is reachable from a web view. Design:
`agents/superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-lametric-design.md`.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.
Each extension is defined in the Appendix or resolved via its URI.

| Term        | URI                            |
|-------------|--------------------------------|
| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`       |
| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`       |
| `test`      | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`       |
| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0`  |

## Types

```mdl
type GoalData
  desc: A determinate progress bar the device draws itself.
  fields:
    start: number
    current: number
    end: number
    unit: string
```

```mdl
type Frame
  desc: One screen in the device's cycle. LaMetric's own shape, not ours — either text or a goal.
  literals: TextFrame | GoalFrame
```

```mdl
type Payload
  desc: The whole body of a widget update; the device replaces its cycle with these frames.
  fields:
    frames: Frame[]
```

```mdl
type TransportConfig
  desc: Where and how to push. An address selects the LAN device; its absence selects the cloud.
  fields:
    address?: string
    port?: number                 # defaults to 4343 for https, 8080 for http
    scheme?: http | https
    appId?: string
    widgetId?: string
    accessToken?: string
```

```mdl
type PushStatus
  desc: What the last push did, published so a surface can show a failure only the user can fix.
  fields:
    state: idle | pushed | failed
    kind?: local | cloud          # pushed only
    status?: number               # failed only
```

## Components

```mdl
component VirtualLaMetric
  desc: On-screen replica of the 37x8 matrix, rasterised by the same pure function as the tests, so
    what is reviewed is what the hardware shows — including whether a line has to scroll.
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: What the device shows

  req F-1.1: While any task is pending or running, a determinate task renders as a goal frame and the
    device draws its own bar.
  req F-1.2: A task reporting no total renders as a text frame, because `goalData` requires an end.
  req F-1.3: With nothing running, the frames show the space's object, feed, type and plugin counts.
  req F-1.4: At most four frames are pushed; the device cycles at a fixed rate, so a longer list
    delays every number in it.
  req F-1.5: Statistic labels are abbreviated at the source. Nine characters fit on 37 pixels, and a
    dashboard is read at a glance.
  req F-1.6: Favorites are not rendered: with no bindable input a favorite is a name scrolling past.
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Transport

  req F-2.1: A configured device address is preferred over the cloud — it avoids a round-trip through
    LaMetric's servers and works with no internet.
  req F-2.2: Both paths issue their request through Tauri's HTTP client. LaMetric's cloud answers a
    CORS preflight with 405 and sends no `Access-Control-*` header, and the LAN device is plain HTTP
    or a self-signed certificate, so neither is reachable from the web view.
  req F-2.3: The permission is scoped to the LaMetric hosts, not granted globally.
  req F-2.4: Incomplete configuration builds no transport and issues no request, which is the common
    case since most users have no device.
  req F-2.5: A rejected push is reported through `PushStatus` and logged, never thrown at the caller.
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Push rate

  req F-3.1: A payload identical to the last one is not sent.
  req F-3.2: Changes inside the minimum interval are coalesced and the last one is sent on the
    trailing edge, so the device ends up showing the latest state rather than the start of a burst.
  req F-3.3: The driver runs with no surface rendered, so the display stays live with no panel open.
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Source of the projection

  req F-4.1: The space is projected by `plugin-space`'s dashboard capability, so one set of queries
    serves every attached peripheral rather than one set per device plugin.
  req F-4.2: This plugin applies its own geometry — the frame count — to those facts.
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
test T-1: A running task takes over the display
  given: a determinate progress task at 45.6% and a space with statistics
  then: one goal frame is pushed with current 46, end 100, and no statistic frames
```

```mdl
test T-2: An indeterminate task still says something
  given: a progress task reporting a current count but no total
  then: a text frame carries the task's title and count, and no goal frame is pushed
```

```mdl
test T-3: Statistics fit without scrolling
  given: a space with 42 objects
  then: the frame reads "42 obj", which is narrower than the 37-pixel matrix
```

```mdl
test T-4: The device is only addressed when it is configured
  given: an address but no access token
  then: no transport is built and no request is made
```

```mdl
test T-5: A burst of space changes becomes one push
  given: three different payloads inside the minimum push interval
  then: the first is sent immediately and only the last of the remainder is sent on the trailing edge
```

```mdl
test T-6: The request is what the device expects
  given: a stand-in HTTP device and a configured local transport
  then: it receives POST /api/v1/dev/widget/update/<app>/<widget> with the access token header and
    the frames as its body
```

